@Elements
The element definitions menu can be used to define the kinds of pieces that
can be placed into boards. Note that you must also associate behaviours with
them by using the assembler, which is documented in a separate file.

:list;Element list

On the list screen, select the element you want to edit; there are 256
possible elements. Super ZZ Zero assumes that element 0 is always used to
mean an empty space, so you should define it as such. Elements 240 to 255
also are reserved for special meanings, although you can still use them for
your own purposes if you are not using the features that use them for their
special meanings or if their attributes are used in the appropriate way for
the special features that use those element numbers.
!num;Special element numbers
:copy
The "Copy attributes" submenu can be used for copying the attributes of one
element to several others in case you want to define several elements that
have the same or similar attributes. (Names are not copied.)

"Source" is the element whose attributes you want copied. "Low target" and
"High target" are the lowest and highest numbers of the elements that you
want the attributes to be copied to. If "Exclude if already defined" is
selected (by default it is selected), then it will not modify any elements
that already have a name.

:at;Attributes

Name: Set the name of this element. This must be filled in or else the
element definition will not be saved; if you erase it, then the attributes
for this element will be lost when the world file is saved. It can only
include letters, digits, and underscores, and cannot start with a digit.

Class: A class number from 0 to 15. Although you can use this in your own
code for your own purposes, its primary purpose is to define the types of
terrains that other objects can be moved into; see "Movement classes".

Pushable: Define which direction it can be pushed.

Crushable: If selected, anything that can crush (which includes objects
being directly pushed), that tries to move into this object, will destroy
it if it cannot be pushed out of the way.

Floor: Allows other objects to be moved onto this one. Normally, a blank
space should have this attribute set, although it is not actually required.

Sensor: Means that this element is a sensor. Tiles with stats may be able
to move on sensors even if the sensor does not have the "Floor" attribute.
See event.doc for details about sensor movement and how to program the
sensor event.

Transportable: Allows this element to be transported through a transporter.

Permanent: Prevents the tile from being overwritten by several things, such
as #PUT, #CHANGE, SINK, DROP, and TELE (in the under layer).

Movement classes: Defines which classes of other objects this object is
normally allowed to move into (for any reason, including pushing, crushing,
transporting, and moving across floors and other terrains); even if the
movement is allowed according to classes, the other attributes will still
be checked as well before the movement will be allowed to proceed. It is
possible for assembly code to override movement classes in specific
circumstances, and this is mandatory for classes 8 to 15.

User-defined: Super ZZ Zero has no use for these attributes, but you can
refer to them in your own assembly code for your own purposes.

:ap;Appearance

Background of under layer: If this tile has a black background colour, then
the background colour from the under layer is displayed instead.

Visible in dark: Suppresses the overlay display of any tile where this
element is in the main layer at the same coordinates.

Line joining class: A number from 0 to 3. This is used for line joining,
if the type of character display is set to "Line joining"; see below for
details about how this works. (This is unrelated to movement classes.)

Own color: Uses the colour of the tile itself.

Over layer: Uses the colour of the overlay at the same coordinates.

Under layer: Uses the colour of the tile underneath at the same coordinates.

Screen data: Uses the colour defined in the screen, at the position where it
will be displayed.

Fixed: Displays a fixed character.

Parameter (character): The parameter of the tile is the code of the character
to be displayed. You may optionally specify a base character; this is added
to the parameter to determine the character to display (modulo 256).

Parameter (mapped): Displays a character from the appearance mapping, using
the parameter of the tile to decide what to display. You must specify an
appearance mapping offset that is a multiple of two. Specify how many bits
of the parameter to use and how many low bits to ignore (i.e. the number of
bits to shift right); this is added to the appearance mapping offset to find
which character should be displayed. You may also optionally specify an
animation to use (either animation 0 or animation 1 can be used), which will
contain an additional number to add to the calculated appearance mapping
(using both the coordinates and the frame counter, and will decide what
number to add according to the definition of that animation).

Animation (ignore parameter): Select one of the four animations, and the
appearance mapping offset to add (which must be a multiple of four). Normally
only the coordinates of the tile are used to decide which animation step will
be displayed, but if "Time-based" is selected, then the frame counter will be
used in addition to the coordinates of the tile.

Line joining: Select an appearance mapping offset which must be a multiple of
sixteen. You can specify which line joining classes to look for in the
adjacent grid cells; "Join edge" means if it is next to the outer edge of
the board grid. To the appearance mapping offset, adds the following numbers
according to which adjacent cells have those line joining classes: 1=east,
2=north, 4=west, 8=south. For example, if the line joining class is 1 and
"Join class 1" is selected, and the tiles to the north and to the west are
also line joining class 1, and the appearance mapping offset is 16x2, then
the final calculated index will be 32+2+4=38.

Misc1/Misc2/Misc3: Display one of the Misc values of the stat. If this tile
is not a stat, then the default character will be displayed.

Over layer: Show the character from the overlay. (If you also want the colour
of the overlay, then you should select that in the colour list as well.)

Under layer: Show the character of the tile underneath this one. (If you also
want the colour, then you should select that in the colour list as well.)

Screen data: Show the character of the screen data. (If you also want the
colour, then you should select that in the colour list as well.)

Counter-based random: Displays a character using a pseudo-random number
generator which only depends on the position and the board number, meaning
that it will remain consistent and will not change over time. Select the
appearance mapping offset which must be a multiple of four, and the type
of distribution to use. They correspond to four different probability
distributions, where 0 is uniform and higher numbers are less uniform.
The alternate distribution additionally alters the distribution according
to the tile coordinates, which also makes it less uniform.

See also:
!-appmap;Appearance mapping
!-anima;Animations

:num;Special element numbers
These special element numbers have the special uses described below. They
are otherwise ordinary elements and can be used like any others.

0: When a tile must be replaced by an empty space, this element is used.
This includes when moving off of a floor, the floor is moved from the
under layer to the main layer, and the under layer in the tile that it
moved from is set to element 0. In the board editor, it is filled with
this element by default.

240: This element is used for overlay tiles, although not all attributes
and events are meaningful (and some attributes are overridden by the SOLID
bit of the overlay tile). The appearance is never used.

241-243: The stat event of these elements is used for independent stats.
The other events and attributes are not used for this purpose.

244: Used when an element number is required for an item definition, but
no item is present in the slot that is being checked. This does not apply
to the use of the ITEM opcode.

255: Some functions that use element numbers will use element 255 as the
default if no other element is specified.
